WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.413
In April 2019, astronomers released a
blurry orange ring on a black background,

00:00:06.413 --> 00:00:09.915
and called it the first image of a black
hole.

00:00:10.195 --> 00:00:16.521
It sits at the centre of a galaxy called
M87, about 55 million light-years away,

00:00:16.521 --> 00:00:21.029
and it weighs about 6.5 billion times as
much as the Sun.

00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:26.536
Three years later, they did it again, this
time for the black hole at the centre of

00:00:26.536 --> 00:00:27.481
our own galaxy.

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.982
But there is an obvious problem.

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:31.990
A black hole traps light.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:37.114
So what exactly is in these pictures, how
do you build a telescope sharp enough to

00:00:37.114 --> 00:00:40.836
take them, and why was the black hole next
door the harder one?

00:00:41.736 --> 00:00:44.024
Start with what the image is not.

00:00:44.304 --> 00:00:47.436
It is not a photograph of the black hole
itself.

00:00:47.716 --> 00:00:51.125
Nothing comes back from inside the event
horizon.

00:00:51.405 --> 00:00:56.472
What you are seeing is the light around
it: hot gas swirling near the black hole,

00:00:56.472 --> 00:01:01.352
glowing in radio waves, with its light
bent by the black hole's gravity into a

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:01.665
ring.

00:01:01.945 --> 00:01:04.807
The dark patch in the middle is called the
shadow.

00:01:05.087 --> 00:01:09.555
It is the region where light that would
have reached us has been bent away or

00:01:09.555 --> 00:01:10.077
captured.

00:01:10.357 --> 00:01:14.913
The shadow is the closest an image can get
to the black hole itself.

00:01:15.193 --> 00:01:17.905
And the event horizon is smaller still.

00:01:18.185 --> 00:01:23.981
For M87, it is about two and a half times
smaller than the shadow, but still just

00:01:23.981 --> 00:01:26.485
under 40 billion kilometres across.

00:01:27.385 --> 00:01:32.337
The idea that gravity bends light is older
than any radio telescope.

00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:38.809
Einstein's general relativity predicted
it, and in 1919, expeditions to a total

00:01:38.809 --> 00:01:44.687
solar eclipse tested the theory by
checking whether starlight passing close

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:46.255
to the Sun was bent.

00:01:46.535 --> 00:01:48.267
It was, as predicted.

00:01:48.547 --> 00:01:52.205
A black hole is that same effect taken to
the extreme.

00:01:52.485 --> 00:01:57.785
Near the event horizon, gravity bends
light so strongly that it can wrap around

00:01:57.785 --> 00:02:02.883
the black hole, which is why the glowing
gas appears as a ring rather than a

00:02:02.883 --> 00:02:03.353
smudge.

00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:08.683
One of the project's lead scientists
called the shadow something predicted by

00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:12.618
Einstein's general relativity that we had
never seen before.

00:02:12.898 --> 00:02:17.692
A century after starlight was first seen
bending around the Sun, we could finally

00:02:17.692 --> 00:02:20.770
see the edge of the place where light
cannot escape.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.103
The second question is how you take a
picture of something so far away.

00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:28.797
The ring is tiny on the sky.

00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:33.958
And the sharpness of any telescope depends
on how wide it is compared with the

00:02:33.958 --> 00:02:35.397
wavelength it observes.

00:02:35.677 --> 00:02:40.415
For radio waves, that means a dish far
bigger than anything we could build.

00:02:40.695 --> 00:02:45.430
To resolve this ring, you would need a
telescope roughly the size of the Earth.

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.288
So astronomers built one, virtually.

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:55.031
The Event Horizon Telescope linked eight
radio observatories around the world, from

00:02:55.031 --> 00:03:01.260
Chile to Hawaii to the South Pole, and
observed together in 2017 at a wavelength

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.739
of 1.3 millimetres.

00:03:03.019 --> 00:03:08.516
By recording the same signals at precisely
timed moments and combining them later,

00:03:08.516 --> 00:03:12.606
the network behaves like a single dish as
wide as the planet.

00:03:12.886 --> 00:03:15.274
The sharpness is hard to picture.

00:03:15.554 --> 00:03:20.996
ESO compares it to reading a newspaper in
New York from a café in Paris.

00:03:21.276 --> 00:03:23.946
The data volumes were just as extreme.

00:03:24.226 --> 00:03:29.762
Each telescope produced about 350
terabytes a day, and supercomputers in

00:03:29.762 --> 00:03:34.145
Germany and the United States combined it
into one image.

00:03:34.425 --> 00:03:38.458
More than 200 researchers worked on that
first result.

00:03:38.738 --> 00:03:40.429
And it was not a one-off.

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:46.248
The same ring showed up across multiple
independent observations, which is part of

00:03:46.248 --> 00:03:51.449
why the team was confident it was real and
not an artefact of the processing.

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:57.357
Now the black hole at the centre of the
Milky Way, called Sagittarius A star.

00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:03.850
It is only about 27,000 light-years away,
and about four million times the mass of

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:04.456
the Sun.

00:04:04.736 --> 00:04:06.573
Closer should mean easier.

00:04:06.853 --> 00:04:07.781
It wasn't.

00:04:08.061 --> 00:04:14.767
Sagittarius A star is more than a thousand
times smaller and less massive than the

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:15.667
one in M87.

00:04:15.947 --> 00:04:21.517
Around the giant in M87, gas takes days or
weeks to complete an orbit.

00:04:21.797 --> 00:04:25.971
Around our much smaller black hole, it
completes an orbit in minutes.

00:04:26.251 --> 00:04:30.615
So the source kept changing while the
telescopes were watching, a bit like

00:04:30.615 --> 00:04:33.327
trying to photograph a puppy chasing its
tail.

00:04:33.607 --> 00:04:38.902
The team had to analyse many images and
average them to reveal the underlying

00:04:38.902 --> 00:04:39.590
structure.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:46.554
The result, published in May 2022 by more
than 300 researchers, looks strikingly

00:04:46.554 --> 00:04:48.141
like the first one.

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:53.750
Seen from Earth, it is about the same size
on the sky as a doughnut sitting on the

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:54.075
Moon.

00:04:54.975 --> 00:04:57.797
So why does a blurry ring matter so much?

00:04:58.077 --> 00:05:01.967
Because the size of the shadow depends on
the black hole's mass.

00:05:02.247 --> 00:05:05.550
The more massive the black hole, the
larger the shadow.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:11.865
For M87, that let the team measure its
enormous mass directly from the image.

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:17.699
And for our own black hole, the ring came
out almost exactly the size Einstein's

00:05:17.699 --> 00:05:19.713
general relativity predicted.

00:05:19.993 --> 00:05:25.373
One of the researchers put it simply:
general relativity governs these objects

00:05:25.373 --> 00:05:25.994
up close.

00:05:26.274 --> 00:05:31.971
Two black holes, a thousand times apart in
mass, look remarkably similar near their

00:05:31.971 --> 00:05:32.383
edges.

00:05:32.663 --> 00:05:37.559
Whatever differences we see further out
come from the material swirling around

00:05:37.559 --> 00:05:40.196
them, not from the black holes themselves.

00:05:41.096 --> 00:05:46.061
In a few years, astronomers went from
drawing black holes to photographing them.

00:05:46.341 --> 00:05:51.365
Not the black hole itself, which no light
can leave, but the ring of light at its

00:05:51.365 --> 00:05:53.164
edge and the shadow it casts.

00:05:53.444 --> 00:05:58.709
If you want more space explained with real
images and real numbers, subscribe.

00:05:58.989 --> 00:06:02.935
Every source and image credit is linked in
the description.
